WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.880
 - Welcome to Internet Technology and Security on Udemy.

00:00:03.880 --> 00:00:05.960
 My name is Charles Severance.

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:06.800
 I'm a professor

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:09.800
 at the University of Michigan School of Information,

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:11.960
 and I've been teaching online programming

00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:16.960
 and technology courses to millions of students since 1997.

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:20.840
 So why take a course on Internet Technology and Security?

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:25.200
 Well, being a great programmer is not just about programming.

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:28.640
 You must also understand context,

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:30.720
 especially in the age of AI.

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:34.480
 The best programmers, the master programmers,

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:38.120
 understand the environment in which software operates.

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:40.400
 They know more than language syntax.

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:42.200
 They know more than algorithms.

00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:44.080
 They know more than libraries.

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:49.000
 They understand systems, networks, history, architecture,

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:53.280
 and the human problems that software is trying to solve.

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:54.680
 I've created a series of courses

00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:56.200
 that provide a straightforward path

00:00:56.200 --> 00:00:59.080
 to becoming one of those master programmers.

00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:04.080
 But for me, that path always starts with history and context

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:08.600
 and always with an eye toward the human side of technology.

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:12.200
 My courses on Internet History, Technology, and Security

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:15.120
 provide a gentle introduction to the technical details

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:18.040
 of the internet and networked applications.

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:22.400
 And don't worry, there is no math and there is no programming.

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:25.920
 Instead, we focus on understanding ideas.

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:29.600
 We learn about packets, sockets, protocols,

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:31.760
 and layered network architectures,

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:33.680
 and we do it in a fun way.

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:36.520
 I present all this material alongside some of my interviews

00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:39.080
 with many of the people who actually helped build

00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:40.840
 the early internet.

00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:43.320
 You get a sense of the 40-year research effort

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:45.960
 that led to the internet that we all use today.

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:48.960
 Frankly, as I designed it, I wanted this course

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:52.320
 to feel more like a learning Netflix series.

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:55.520
 Put it on your television, grab some snacks,

00:01:55.520 --> 00:01:58.920
 sit back, and binge watch lectures and interviews

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:00.840
 about how the internet works.

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:05.640
 There are no stressful deadlines, no essays, no pressure.

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:09.080
 Just relax, enjoy the material, and learn.

00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:11.360
 My goal is to start you on the path

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:13.080
 toward becoming a master programmer

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:16.040
 through enjoyable courses where you'll learn a great deal

00:02:16.040 --> 00:02:17.880
 without making your head hurt.

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:21.000
 And at the end of the course, I'll talk to you again

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:22.800
 about the road ahead and the next steps

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:25.120
 you might take on your journey.

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:29.640
 But for now, just sit back, relax, and enjoy the course.

00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:31.680
 Cheers, see you soon.

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:34.680
 (upbeat jazz music)

